  "outline": [
    "The four basic shapes first, in the corner: e^x, e^(-x) (minus in front of x flips in the y-axis), -e^x (minus in front of the graph flips in the x-axis), -e^(-x) (both minuses flip twice).",
    "The question (line 0), empty axes at the side. Three steps pinned: shape, shift up, intercepts — the graph drawn as we go.",
    "Step one, shape: it is the minus sign that matters; the minus is in front of x, so the shape of e^(-x) (line 1), the e, minus and x lit in the question, lifted from the corner onto the axes. The 2 and the 3 only stretch it (the curve stretches into 2e^(-3x)); a note under the 2.",
    "Step two, shift up (1081.08 'draw the basic curve first, then shift it by c'): as x grows 2e^(-3x) -> 0 (line 2), dashed y = 0; so y -> 0 + 1 (line 3); +1 shifts the whole graph up by one — the curve and the dashed asymptote slide up, asymptote y = 1.",
    "Step three, intercepts, the FULL equation: Sub x = 0: y = 2e^0 + 1 = 2(1) + 1 = 3, mark (0, 3) on the curve. Sub y = 0: 2e^(-3x) + 1 = 0, e^(-3x) = -1/2 (no solutions) — a power of e is always positive — no x-intercept.",
    "Last: the sketch in full — the shape of e^(-x) through (0, 3), above the asymptote y = 1."
  ],
